 ## Overview
-Octree-Based Dual Contouring (3D) is a voxel-to-mesh extraction method that uses an octree to adaptively subdivide space, placing dual vertices at locations with the smallest reconstruction error [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Dual Contouring with Octree](1271-dual-contouring-with-octree.md) by introducing adaptive subdivision and a simplification phase that collapses internal nodes into pseudo-nodes when the Quadratic Error Function (QEF) error remains below a threshold, thereby reducing mesh complexity [S1, unofficial].
+Octree-Based Dual Contouring (3D) is a voxel-to-mesh extraction method that uses an octree to adaptively subdivide space, placing dual vertices at locations with the smallest reconstruction error [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Dual Contouring with Octree](1271-dual-contouring-with-octree.md) by introducing adaptive subdivision and a simplification phase that collapses internal nodes into pseudo-nodes when the Quadratic Error Function (QEF) error remains below a threshold, thereby reducing mesh complexity [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Manifold Dual Contouring](2138-manifold-dual-contouring.md) by prioritizing adaptive spatial subdivision over strict manifold guarantees, and from [Adaptive Contouring via Octree Vertex Clustering](2142-adaptive-contouring-via-octree-vertex-clustering.md) by using QEF-based vertex placement rather than geometric clustering.
 
